# A MISSING hashed asset must 404. It must never fall through to the SPA rule
# below and be answered with index.html + HTTP 200.
#
# That combination is what took dev.utahpros.app down on 2026-07-27. During a
# deployment swap a Cloudflare PoP requested a chunk that was not uploaded yet,
# the catch-all answered with index.html at status 200, and the `/assets/*`
# rule in public/_headers stamped it `immutable, max-age=31536000`. Both the
# edge and the browser then cached HTML under a .js URL for a year. A browser
# refuses text/html for <script type="module">, so the entry graph never
# instantiates: main.jsx never runs, React never mounts, and NOTHING throws.
# A permanently blank page with no error and no in-app way out.
#
# Static files take precedence over these rules (proven live: the catch-all
# below has always existed, yet real /assets/*.js are served as themselves), so
# this only affects assets that genuinely are not there.
#
# A clean 404 is recoverable — the module fails loudly, the boot guard in
# index.html sees it and bounces through /reset. A poisoned 200 is not.
/app-assets/* /asset-missing.html 404
/assets/*     /asset-missing.html 404

# SPA routing. Every non-file path renders the app shell.
/* /index.html 200
